/* Ported from MicroLab Quest (Avicenna lectures 3 & 4). Topic ids map to m04 nodes. */
window.MICRO_TOPICS=[
 {id:"gen", lec:3, name:"Genetic elements of bacteria", html:`
  <p>Four structures carry bacterial properties: <b>chromosome, plasmids, transposons, bacteriophages</b>.</p>
  <div class="tscroll"><table><tr><th>Plasmids</th><th>Transposons</th><th>Bacteriophages</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Circular, <b>double-stranded</b>, <b>extrachromosomal</b> DNA, smaller than the chromosome. Transferred by conjugation, transformation, transduction.</td>
  <td><b>Jumping genes</b>. Move (1) within the same chromosome or (2) chromosome ↔ plasmid.</td>
  <td>Viruses that infect bacteria. <b>Head</b> = protein capsid with DNA or RNA. <b>Tail</b> = hollow core + contractile sheath; <b>base plate</b> with tail fibers that adhere to specific cell-wall receptors.</td></tr></table></div>
  <p><b>Functions of plasmids</b></p><ul>
  <li><b>F plasmids</b>: sex pilus formation and gene transfer by <span class="hl">conjugation</span>.</li>
  <li><b>Bacteriocins</b>: antibiotic-like substances made by bacteria to kill other bacteria.</li>
  <li><b>Antibiotic production</b>: a few antibiotic-synthesis gene clusters sit on <i>Streptomyces</i> plasmids (methylenomycin on plasmid SCP1). The <b>streptomycin</b> genes are chromosomal, not plasmid-borne.</li>
  <li><b>Virulence plasmids</b>: <b>R plasmids</b> = antibiotic resistance; <b>Tox plasmids</b> = toxin production.</li></ul>`},
 {id:"var", lec:3, name:"Variation, mutation & gene transfer", html:`
  <div class="tscroll"><table><tr><th>Phenotypic variation</th><th>Genotypic variation</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Environmental change, no change in genes. <b>Reversible, not heritable</b>. Examples: L-forms; loss of flagella on exposure to phenol.</td>
  <td>Change in genetic constitution. <b>Irreversible, heritable</b>. Causes: mutation; gene transfer (transformation, transduction, conjugation).</td></tr></table></div>
  <p><b>Mutation</b> = change in DNA nucleotide sequence. <b>Spontaneous</b> (replication error) or <b>induced</b> (radiation, chemicals).</p>
  <p><b>Gene transfer</b></p><ul>
  <li><b>Conjugation</b>: DNA (plasmid) passes cell to cell through the <span class="hl">sex pilus</span>.</li>
  <li><b>Transformation</b>: <span class="hl">dying bacteria release DNA</span>, taken up by a recipient. Chromosomal or plasmid origin.</li>
  <li><b>Transduction</b>: by <span class="hl">bacteriophages</span>. <b>Lytic cycle</b> ends with lysis, death of host, release of new phages. <b>Lysogenic cycle</b>: phage DNA integrates into the chromosome (= <b>prophage</b>) and divides with it; the bacterium gains new properties, e.g. <b>diphtheria bacilli make toxin only when lysogenized</b>.</li></ul>`},
 {id:"mech", lec:3, name:"Antimicrobials: definitions & mechanisms", html:`
  <ul><li><b>Bactericidal</b> = kill; <b>bacteriostatic</b> = inhibit multiplication.</li>
  <li><b>Antibiotics</b> were originally natural products of molds (<b>Penicillium</b>) and bacteria (<b>Streptomyces</b>); <b>chemotherapeutic agents</b> were chemically synthesized. The split is historical: most agents in use now are semisynthetic or fully synthetic, and "antibiotic" is used for all of them.</li>
  <li><b>Broad spectrum</b> = Gram + and Gram −. <b>Narrow</b> = only one of them.</li></ul>
  <div class="tscroll"><table><tr><th>Target</th><th>Drugs</th><th>Key point</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Cell wall synthesis</td><td><b>Penicillin, cephalosporins</b></td><td>Bactericidal, minimal toxicity: human cells have <span class="hl">no cell wall</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td>Cell membrane</td><td><b>Polymyxins</b></td><td><span class="hl">Narrow margin</span> of selective toxicity</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Protein synthesis (ribosome)</td><td><b>Aminoglycosides, tetracycline</b></td><td>Bacteria <span class="hl">70S</span>, human <span class="hl">80S</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td>Nucleic acid synthesis</td><td><b>Ciprofloxacin</b> / <b>Metronidazole</b></td><td>Cipro inhibits <span class="hl">DNA gyrase</span> (topoisomerase II); metronidazole is <span class="hl">reduced inside anaerobes</span> to radicals that break DNA, which is why it is an anti-anaerobe drug</td></tr></table></div>`},
 {id:"use", lec:3, name:"Ideal antibiotic, combinations, prophylaxis, empiric therapy", html:`
  <p><b>Ideal antibiotic (7)</b>: selective toxicity · bactericidal rather than bacteriostatic · <b>narrow spectrum</b> (less induction of resistance) · easily administered · effective level at the site of infection · low allergenic potential · safe for children, pregnant and lactating women. No real drug meets all seven, so the list is a comparison tool, not a description of any product.</p>
  <p><b>Combinations</b>: a single agent is preferred whenever one will do the job (fewer side effects, less flora disturbance). Two or more when: serious infection of unknown cause (sepsis in the immunocompromised) · synergistic effect · prevent emergence of resistance (tuberculosis is the standing example) · mixed infections.</p>
  <p><b>Chemoprophylaxis</b> = antibiotic given to prevent infection:</p><ul>
  <li><b>Penicillin</b> → recurrent pharyngitis in patients who had <span class="hl">rheumatic fever</span>.</li>
  <li><b>Amoxicillin 2 g orally, single dose 30–60 min before the procedure</b> → endocarditis by <span class="hl">viridans group streptococci</span>. Under <b>AHA 2021</b> only the <b>highest-risk</b> hearts qualify: prosthetic valve or prosthetic material used for valve repair, previous infective endocarditis, specified unrepaired or recently repaired congenital heart disease, and cardiac transplant with valvulopathy. "Any damaged valve" is <b>not</b> an indication. Covered procedures are those that manipulate gingival tissue or the periapical region of a tooth, or perforate the oral mucosa. In penicillin allergy: cephalexin, azithromycin, clarithromycin or doxycycline — <b>clindamycin was removed in the 2021 update</b> because of its adverse-effect profile.</li>
  <li><b>Intrapartum intravenous penicillin G</b> (ampicillin as the alternative), given <b>to the mother in labour</b> → early-onset neonatal <span class="hl">group B streptococcal</span> sepsis and meningitis. The newborn is not the one dosed.</li>
  <li><b>Amoxicillin</b> before <b>dental implant placement</b> → a single preoperative dose reduces early implant failure. This is surgical-site prophylaxis and is unrelated to the cardiac indication above.</li>
  <li><b>Rifampin</b> (or ciprofloxacin, or ceftriaxone) → close contacts of a case of <span class="hl">meningococcal</span> disease.</li></ul>
  <p><b>Empiric therapy</b> = "best guess" antibiotic <b>before sensitivity results</b>. Based on likely bacteria at that site. Indicated for <b>severe oral and dental infections</b> (risk of spread via lymph/blood → septicemia). Start <span class="hl">without delay, but after collecting specimens for culture</span>.</p>`},
 {id:"comp", lec:3, name:"Complications of antibiotics", html:`
  <ol><li><b>Toxicity</b>: <b>tetracycline</b> → intrinsic staining and enamel hypoplasia of teeth still forming, so it is avoided <b>under 8 years</b> and from the second trimester of pregnancy · <b>streptomycin</b> → 8th cranial nerve → deafness · <b>aminoglycosides</b> → nephrotoxicity <b>and</b> ototoxicity · <b>chloramphenicol</b> → bone marrow depression.</li>
  <li><b>Allergy</b>: <b>penicillin</b> → urticaria, anaphylactic shock, serum sickness · local <b>sulphonamides</b> → contact dermatitis.</li>
  <li><b>Resistant strains</b> from <b>abuse</b>: low dose, interrupted course, no real indication, improper choice → resistant mutants overgrow. Hence: do in-vitro susceptibility testing.</li>
  <li><b>Superinfection</b>: resistant normal flora overgrow when sensitive flora are killed. <b>Pseudomembranous colitis</b> (<span class="hl">Clostridioides difficile</span>, long known as <i>Clostridium difficile</i>) · <b>oral thrush</b> (<span class="hl">Candida</span>).</li></ol>`},
 {id:"tox", lec:3, name:"Exotoxin vs endotoxin", html:`
  <div class="tscroll"><table><tr><th></th><th>Exotoxin</th><th>Endotoxin</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Source</td><td>Secreted by living organisms, mainly Gm+ (also Gm−)</td><td>Part of <b>Gm− cell wall</b>, released on cell disintegration</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Coding gene</td><td><b>Often plasmids or bacteriophages</b>; some are chromosomal</td><td><b>Chromosome</b></td></tr>
  <tr><td>Antigenicity</td><td>Highly antigenic</td><td>Poorly antigenic</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Nature</td><td>Protein</td><td><b>LPS (lipid A)</b></td></tr>
  <tr><td>Heat</td><td>Unstable</td><td>Stable</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Toxoid</td><td>Can be converted</td><td>Cannot</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Specificity</td><td>Specific action per toxin</td><td>Non-specific: all give fever and shock</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Toxicity</td><td>High</td><td>Low</td></tr></table></div>`},
 {id:"ss", lec:4, name:"Staphylococcus vs Streptococcus", html:`
  <div class="tscroll"><table><tr><th>Staphylococcus</th><th>Streptococcus</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Grape-like <b>clusters</b></td><td><b>Chains</b> or pairs</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Catalase +</b></td><td><b>Catalase −</b></td></tr>
  <tr><td>Facultative anaerobes</td><td>Obligate &amp; facultative anaerobes, grow in 5–10% CO₂</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Grow on ordinary medium (nutrient agar)</td><td><b>Fastidious</b>: need enriched medium (blood agar)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Normal flora of skin, nose, mucosa</td><td>Normal flora of throat, colon, female genital tract</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Localized</b> infections</td><td>Infections tend to <b>spread</b></td></tr>
  <tr><td>Resistant to drying; <b>most strains now resist penicillin</b> (β-lactamase)</td><td>Delicate; pyogenes and the viridans group remain <b>largely penicillin sensitive</b> (pneumococcal resistance does occur)</td></tr></table></div>
  <p>The <b>catalase test</b> separates the two genera. The <b>coagulase test</b> splits staphylococci into coagulase-positive (<b>S. aureus</b>) and coagulase-negative (<b>S. epidermidis, S. saprophyticus</b>).</p>`},
 {id:"aur", lec:4, name:"Staphylococcus aureus", html:`
  <p>The most virulent and medically important staphylococcus. It is the leading cause of <b>skin and soft-tissue abscess</b>, <b>osteomyelitis</b> and <b>acute bacterial endocarditis</b>, and the cause of staphylococcal food poisoning and toxic shock syndrome. It is <b>not</b> the commonest cause of bacterial infection in general. <b>Beta hemolytic</b>, <b>salt tolerant (grows in 7.5–10% NaCl, the basis of mannitol salt agar)</b>, tolerates drying, <b>golden yellow pigment</b>, non-sporing, non-motile.</p>
  <p><b>Toxins</b></p><ul>
  <li><b>Hemolysins</b> lyse RBCs · <b>Leucocidins</b> kill leucocytes.</li>
  <li><b>Enterotoxins A–E</b>: heat and acid resistant → <span class="hl">food poisoning</span>.</li>
  <li><b>TSST-1</b> (historically called enterotoxin F) → toxic shock syndrome. It is a <b>superantigen</b>: it cross-links MHC II to the T-cell receptor without specific antigen, so a huge fraction of T cells fire at once → cytokine storm, rash, desquamation, multi-organ failure.</li>
  <li><b>Epidermolytic (exfoliative) toxin A &amp; B</b> → <span class="hl">scalded skin syndrome</span> in young children.</li></ul>
  <p><b>Enzymes</b>: catalase · coagulase · <b>hyaluronidase</b> and <b>fibrinolysin (staphylokinase)</b> = spreading factors · <b>β-lactamase</b> (β-lactam resistance) · nuclease · lipase.</p>
  <p><b>Cell-associated</b>: <b>Protein A</b> (antiphagocytic) · <b>clumping factor</b> (clumping in plasma, inhibits phagocytosis) · <b>slime layer / capsule</b> (inhibits chemotaxis and phagocytosis; attaches to artificial surfaces).</p>
  <p><b>Diseases</b>: non-invasive = food poisoning · cutaneous = SSSS, impetigo, folliculitis, furuncles (boils), abscess · systemic = TSS, bacteremia, endocarditis, pneumonia, osteomyelitis.</p>
  <p><b>Lab</b>: wound swab/pus/blood → Gram stain → culture: <b>beta hemolysis</b> on blood agar, <b>golden pigment</b> on nutrient agar, <b>catalase +, coagulase +, mannitol fermentation</b>.</p>`},
 {id:"cons", lec:4, name:"Coagulase-negative staphylococci", html:`
  <ul><li><b>S. epidermidis</b>: major skin flora. <b>Nosocomial, device/implant infections</b> (shunts, catheters, artificial valves and joints, pacemakers).</li>
  <li><b>S. saprophyticus</b>: occasionally on skin, GI and GU tracts. <b>UTI in young women</b>.</li></ul>`},
 {id:"scl", lec:4, name:"Genus Streptococcus & classification", html:`
  <p>Gram + spherical cocci in <b>chains</b>, <b>catalase −</b>, facultative anaerobe. Classified by <b>oxygen requirement</b>, <b>hemolysis on blood agar</b>, and <b>Lancefield</b> (cell-wall <b>C carbohydrate</b> antigen). Groups <b>A</b> and <b>B</b> are the main human pathogens; groups C and G also cause pharyngitis, and the old group D organisms are now the separate genus <b>Enterococcus</b>.</p>
  <ul><li><b>Aerobes</b> (grow on blood agar): <b>α</b> = incomplete, <b>green</b> → viridans group streptococci, Strep. pneumoniae · <b>β</b> = complete → Lancefield grouping, <b>letters A to V omitting I and J, so 20 groups</b> · <b>γ</b> = no hemolysis → <b>Enterococcus faecalis</b>, now classified in its own genus rather than as a streptococcus.</li>
  <li><b>Anaerobes</b>: <b>Peptostreptococci</b>.</li></ul>`},
 {id:"gas", lec:4, name:"Streptococcus pyogenes (group A) & scarlet fever", html:`
  <p>Complete <b>beta</b> hemolysis. <b>Virulence</b>: <b>M protein</b> (adherence to skin, inhibits phagocytosis) · <b>pyrogenic exotoxins SPE A, B, C</b> = scarlet fever toxin · enzymes <b>streptokinase, hyaluronidase</b> (spreading).</p>
  <ul><li><b>Respiratory</b>: pharyngitis ("strep throat"), tonsillitis, scarlet fever, otitis media, sinusitis.</li>
  <li><b>Skin</b>: pyoderma (impetigo), erysipelas, cellulitis, <b>necrotizing fasciitis</b> (flesh-eating, invasive).</li>
  <li><b>Late non-suppurative</b>: <b>acute rheumatic fever</b> only after <span class="hl">pharyngitis</span> (a skin infection does not cause it) · <b>acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis</b> after <span class="hl">pyoderma or pharyngitis</span>, with skin infection the classic antecedent.</li></ul>
  <p><b>Lab</b>: throat swab/pus/blood · GPC in chains · beta hemolytic on BA · <b>catalase −, bacitracin sensitive, penicillin sensitive, ASO titre</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Scarlet fever</b>: the pyrogenic exotoxins act as <b>superantigens</b> → fever, a sandpaper-textured generalized rash, then desquamation, with <b>circumoral pallor</b>. Oral signs: <b>white strawberry tongue</b> that sheds to a <b>red strawberry tongue</b> (deep-red raised papillae), <b>palatal petechiae and punctate erythema of the soft palate</b>, inflamed uvula. (Named "Forchheimer spots" in some older texts, but that sign is classically described in rubella and is not specific.)</p>`},
 {id:"gbs", lec:4, name:"Group B strep & pneumococcus", html:`
  <p><b>S. agalactiae (group B)</b>: flora of lower GIT and female genital tract. Causes <b>neonatal meningitis, puerperal sepsis, pneumonia</b>. Specimens: CSF, blood, vaginal smear, urine. GPC chains, beta hemolytic, catalase −, <b>bacitracin resistant</b>, <b>CAMP test +</b>.</p>
  <p><b>S. pneumoniae</b>: <b>capsulated Gram + diplococci</b>, <b>alpha</b> hemolytic. <b>Otitis media and sinusitis (most common)</b>, pneumococcal pneumonia, meningitis; pericarditis, conjunctivitis, arthritis, peritonitis.</p>`},
 {id:"vir", lec:4, name:"Viridans streptococci & enterococci", html:`
  <p><b>Viridans group</b>: on teeth, throat, colon, female genital tract. <b>No group-specific carbohydrate → not Lancefield-groupable</b>. <b>Alpha-haemolysis</b> leaves a green zone around the colony from haemoglobin breakdown — that green is a change in the agar, not a pigment the organism makes. Cause <b>endocarditis, dental caries, pulpitis</b>. Pulpal and root canal infections are <b>polymicrobial</b>: alpha-haemolytic streptococci and anaerobic Gram-positive cocci (<i>Peptostreptococcus</i>, <i>Parvimonas</i>) are among the organisms most often recovered, and obligate anaerobes dominate once the canal is necrotic.</p>
  <p><b>Enterococci (gamma)</b>: <b>E. faecalis, E. faecium</b>. Flora of GIT and lower genital tract; a separate genus, not streptococci. Nosocomial/opportunistic: <b>UTI, wound infection, endocarditis</b>. <b>E. faecalis</b> is the species most often recovered from <b>root-filled teeth with persistent periapical disease</b>: it survives high pH and many intracanal medicaments. It is one important contributor to <span class="hl">endodontic failure</span>, not the single cause — missed canals, incomplete cleaning and coronal leakage matter at least as much.</p>`},
 {id:"bac", lec:4, name:"Gram-positive bacilli: Bacillus & Lactobacillus", html:`
  <div class="tscroll"><table><tr><th></th><th>Sporing</th><th>Non-sporing</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Aerobes / facultative</td><td><b>Bacillus</b></td><td><b>Lactobacillus, Corynebacterium</b></td></tr>
  <tr><td>Anaerobes</td><td><b>Clostridium</b></td><td><b>Actinomyces</b></td></tr></table></div>
  <p><b>Bacillus anthracis</b>: Gram + aerobic bacilli, endospores. <b>Anthrax</b> (enzootic; bioterrorism agent). Transmission: direct contact with animal products (wool, bone, hair); inoculation of spores through abrasions; inhalation (less common). Virulence: an antiphagocytic <b>poly-D-glutamic acid capsule</b> plus two toxins that share a <b>protective antigen</b> binding subunit — <b>lethal toxin</b> (tissue necrosis) and <b>edema toxin</b> (severe swelling).</p>
  <ul><li><b>Cutaneous</b>: papule → <b>painless, black, swollen malignant pustule</b>; may invade lymph/blood → fatal septicemia.</li>
  <li><b>Pulmonary (wool sorter's disease)</b>: hemorrhagic lymphadenitis and mediastinitis.</li></ul>
  <p><b>Lactobacillus</b>: commensal of mucous membranes. Makes lots of lactic acid (<b>acidogenic</b>), survives very low pH (<b>aciduric</b>). It is a <b>secondary invader</b>, not an initiator: numbers rise once a lesion is established, and it drives progression through <b>dentine in advanced caries</b>. <i>S. mutans</i> is the organism of initiation.</p>`},
 {id:"cor", lec:4, name:"Corynebacterium diphtheriae", html:`
  <p>Gram + small slender <b>pleomorphic</b> rods, non-sporing, clumps like <b>Chinese characters</b>. Spread by <b>droplets</b>, respiratory route.</p>
  <ul><li>Pathogenic strains are <b>lysogenized by bacteriophage</b> → powerful <b>A-B exotoxin</b>. The A fragment <b>ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2</b>, shutting down protein synthesis in the cell it enters → tissue necrosis.</li>
  <li><b>Pseudomembrane</b> in the throat may extend → <b>suffocation</b>.</li>
  <li><b>Toxaemia, not invasion</b>: toxin spreads via blood to <b>heart, muscle, peripheral nerves</b>; neuritis of cranial/peripheral nerves → paralysis, difficulty speaking and swallowing.</li></ul>
  <p><b>Prevention in Canada</b>: diphtheria <b>toxoid</b> inside a DTaP-containing combination vaccine at <b>2, 4, 6 and 18 months</b>, a booster at <b>4–6 years</b>, and Tdap in adolescence with adult boosters; the exact product and timing vary by province. <b>Treatment (immediate!)</b>: <b>antitoxin early</b> — it neutralises only toxin not yet bound to cells, so hours matter — plus penicillin or erythromycin to clear the organism, and respiratory support.</p>`},
 {id:"clo", lec:4, name:"Clostridium & Actinomycetes", html:`
  <p><b>Clostridium</b>: Gram + rods, <b>spores</b>, <b>obligate anaerobes</b>; habitat soil and intestine of animals/humans.</p>
  <ul><li><b>C. perfringens</b>: <b>myonecrosis (gas gangrene)</b>, anaerobic cellulitis. Most important toxin: <b>α toxin (lecithinase)</b> → lyses endothelial cells, RBCs, leucocytes, platelets. Enzymes <b>DNase, collagenase</b> liquefy tissue → spread.</li>
  <li><b>C. tetani</b>: <b>tetanus</b> = spastic paralysis, <b>trismus (lock jaw)</b>, risus sardonicus, painful spasms; death by spasm and paralysis of the respiratory muscles. Toxin <b>tetanospasmin</b>, a <b>plasmid-encoded</b> neurotoxin that travels up the axon and <b>blocks release of the inhibitory transmitters glycine and GABA</b>. Removing inhibition is why the paralysis is spastic, not flaccid.</li></ul>
  <p><b>Actinomycetes</b>: branching filaments like fungi but <b>true bacteria</b>: Gram + type wall; no nuclear membrane or mitochondria; sensitive to antibacterials, not antifungals. Oral: <b>cervicofacial actinomycosis</b>, caries, plaque, implant infections.</p>
  <p><b>Cervicofacial actinomycosis</b>: <b>endogenous</b> (normal oral flora). Risk: untreated caries, extractions, trauma. Face/neck/mandible swelling, fibrosis, <b>draining sinus tracts</b>; pus with <b>yellow sulfur granules</b>, which are colonies of the organism, not true sulfur. Treatment is <b>drainage plus a prolonged course of penicillin</b>, weeks to months, because the fibrosis keeps drug out.</p>`}
];
